    "q-182455": {
      "dom_id": "step-q182455",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$\\,$ If you don’t study, then you won't pass the exam.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't study.\n\n- $B:$ You won't pass the exam.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You study.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You study, or you won't pass the exam.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Study, or you won't pass the exam.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-182455-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "Pass the exam, or study.",
              "html": "Pass the exam, or study."
            },
            {
