    "q-182574": {
      "dom_id": "step-q182574",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "$\\,$ Either apologize, or she won't forgive you.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Apologize.\n\n- $B:$ She won't forgive you.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't apologize.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
